amt\">2</td><td class=\"amt\">7.41%</td></tr><tr><td>/tests/magnesium/</td><td class=\"amt\">4</td><td class=\"amt\">2</td><td class=\"amt\">50.00%</td></tr><tr><td>/tests/vitamin-a/</td><td class=\"amt\">5</td><td class=\"amt\">2</td><td class=\"amt\">40.00%</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">This cart rate is counted differently from the three tables above, and the two are not comparable. Here it is the share of people who viewed that page and added something to the cart, however they arrived. Above, it is cart presses divided by visits that STARTED on that kind of page. A page can convert people it did not bring in, so a page can rank high here and low there.</p>\n  <p class=\"note\"><b>Visits above are engaged sessions</b>, meaning someone actually read\n  the page. Today 6,756 of 13,158 US\n  sessions were non-engaged, almost all of it datacenter crawler traffic from Google Cloud\n  and AWS that loads a page for two seconds and leaves. Counting those would roughly\n  quadruple the denominator and make every rate here look four times worse than reality.</p>\n</section>","notice_html":"<div class=\"notice\"><b>The site stopped recording its checkout events on Aug 29, so every cart and checkout figure below is wrong from that day on.</b> Since Aug 29 purchase, email capture and payment events have logged nothing at all, and cart counts collapsed with them, while Stripe went on taking orders: 82 paid orders landed across those days. Orders, revenue and traffic come from Stripe and from page views, so those are unaffected and can be read normally. Days with no tracking are drawn as gaps, never as zeros. Tracked on GitLab work item 358.</div>","daily":[{"date":"2026-08-29","partial":false,"organic_visits":2732,"engaged_sessions":4206,"checkout_visits":42,"org_checkout_visits":36,"carts":null,"reached_checkout":null,"cart_tracking_down":true},{"date":"2026-08-30","partial":false,"organic_visits":2799,"engaged_sessions":4126,"checkout_visits":51,"org_checkout_v